# About LugalOS

Documentation > About LugalOS

LugalOS is a bare-metal, capability-isolated microkernel operating system built for RISC-V microcontrollers and workstations. Inspired by the minimalism of Plan 9 and the expressiveness of classic Lisp machines, LugalOS combines robust hardware-enforced memory domains with interactive developer ergonomics.

## 1. Architectural Highlights

* Capability-Enforced Microkernel Core: Preemptive multitasking with hardware memory domain isolation--Physical Memory Protection (PMP) on NOMMU microcontrollers, Sv39 page tables on RV64--strictly confining device drivers and user applications.
* Plan 9 IPC & 9P Filesystem Networking: Copy-always message channels (chan_t) form the fundamental IPC primitive. Serves and consumes 9P2000 filesystems over USB CDC-ACM, SLIP serial, and TCP.
* Exact Symbolic Computer Algebra System (CAS): Pure Lisp symbolic mathematics operating without hardware floating-point instructions: arbitrary-precision bignums, exact rationals \mathbb{Q}, Gaussian rationals \mathbb{Q}(i), polynomial factorization, symbolic calculus, and power series.
* Horizontal Tiling Window Ribbon: Multi-window graphical workspace on 7" and 5" LCD panels with smooth inertial scrolling, split canvases, touch gestures, and proportional serif typography.
* On-Device C11 Compilation: Native chibicc compiler generating RISC-V ELF binaries directly on the microcontroller, executed as confined U-mode tasks.
* Zero Floating-Point in Kernel / M-Mode: Boot code enforces mstatus.FS = 0. All mathematics, sensor telemetry scaling, and display coordinates use pure fixed-point or exact integer math.
* Zero SRAM Builtin Footprint: Symbols, primitives, and standard library catalogs reside in Flash .rodata tables sorted for O(\log N) binary search, preserving SRAM for application execution.

## 2. Supported Hardware Platforms

* Raspberry Pi Pico 2 & RP2350-LCD-7 (rp2350-terminal, rp2350): Dual Hazard3 RISC-V cores @ 150 MHz, dual CDC-ACM USB (console + 9P), 8 MB PSRAM, 7" 800x480 RGB LCD.
* Waveshare ESP32-P4-WIFI6-Touch-LCD-7B (esp32p4-lcd7b): Dual-core RV32 @ 400 MHz (rev v3.2), 32 MB DDR PSRAM, 7" 1024x600 MIPI-DSI, GT911 touch, DWC2 USB host, SDIO link to C6 Wi-Fi 6 co-processor.
* M5Stack Tab5 (esp32p4-tab5): Dual-core RV32 @ 400 MHz (rev v1.3), 32 MB PSRAM, 5" 720x1280 portrait IPS with ST7121 TDDI, BMI270 IMU, INA226 fuel gauge, dual-orientation compositor.
* ESP32-P4-NANO (esp32p4): Dual-core RV32 compute node with 100 Mbps RMII Ethernet and SDMMC storage.
* Waveshare ESP32-C6-Zero (esp32c6): 160 MHz RV32IMAC low-power 802.11ax Wi-Fi node and LLP wireless coprocessor.
* QEMU RISC-V Virt Targets (rv32, rv64, rv64-smp): Emulated NOMMU and Sv39 MMU virtual targets for continuous integration.

## 3. On-Device Applications & Tools

* Interactive Shell (lsh): Pipelines, I/O redirection, procfs/devfs navigation, background job control.
* Text Editors: Full-screen Emacs-style editor (e) and teletype line editor (ed).
* Ribbon Reader (reader): Anti-aliased proportional serif document reader for Markdown and Gemtext.
* Chess Computer (chess): Interactive chess engine with Lazy SMP parallel search, opening book, and board display.
* Spartan & Gemini Client (gem): Hypertext browsing over the Spartan protocol and local .gmi files.

## 4. Third-Party Code, Data & Origins

LugalOS itself is licensed under the MIT License. Everything below came from elsewhere, is used under its own license, and is listed here when it is added to the tree.

### Linked into firmware images

* Infineon CYW43439 firmware (rp2350-wifi): the three blobs in firmware/cyw43/ are uploaded to the Wi-Fi chip at every boot. Infineon Permissive Binary License 1.0, reproduced in firmware/cyw43/LICENSE-permissive-binary-license-1.0.txt. See firmware/cyw43/README.md for where they come from.
* Espressif Wi-Fi, PHY and coexistence libraries (ESP32-C6): libpp, libnet80211, libcore, libphy and libcoexist from ESP-IDF (components/espwifi/lib, espphy, espcoex), Apache License 2.0. They run in a confined U-mode domain (cmake/radioesp32c6.cmake, tools/c6radiolibs.py).
* wpasupplicant (ESP32-C6): ESP-IDF's port of hostap's wpasupplicant (BSD license, with Espressif's port under Apache License 2.0), station side only, built into the same radio domain.
* sekigon-gonnoc's Pico-PIO-USB: the three PIO programs of the USB host port (drivers/piousbrp2350.c: usbtxfs, usbnrzidecoder, usbedge_detector, and the low-speed PRE variant of the transmitter's end) are from Pico-PIO-USB (MIT License, quoted in that file's header), embedded as assembled instruction words. The host engine, hub and keyboard code around them are written for this tree.
* Monocypher 4.0.3 by Loup Vaillant, Michael Savage and Fabio Scotoni: X25519, ChaCha20 and Poly1305 for TLS and protected 9P sessions, from Monocypher (dual CC0-1.0 / BSD 2-Clause; used under BSD 2-Clause). Unmodified in third_party/crypto/monocypher/, with LICENCE.md and ORIGIN.md.
* p256-m by Manuel Pegourie-Gonnard: P-256 ECDSA and ECDH for TLS certificates and update-image signatures, from p256-m (Apache License 2.0). Unmodified in third_party/crypto/p256-m/, with LICENSE, apache-2.0.txt and ORIGIN.md.

### Fonts

* Frederic Cambus's Spleen: the 8x16 terminal font (drivers/font8x16.c) is generated from Spleen (BSD 2-Clause License, copied in full to tools/fonts/LICENSE.spleen) by tools/genfontbdf.py. The chess figurines and the replacement glyph are drawn in the generator for this tree.
* Google Noto Sans Mono and Noto Serif: the multi-density font tables (drivers/fontnotomono.c, drivers/fontnotoserif.c) are rendered from the system's Noto fonts by tools/gennotofonts.py (SIL Open Font License 1.1).
* Liberation Serif / Noto Serif: the reader's proportional serif tables (drivers/fontserif.c) are rendered by tools/genserif_font.py from whichever of the two is installed (both SIL Open Font License 1.1).

### Host tools

* Microsoft UF2 Tools: tools/uf2conv.py and tools/uf2families.json are from Microsoft UF2 (MIT License).
* Raspberry Pi Picotool: used for RP2350 flashing, image analysis and partition tables, from the Picotool repository (BSD 3-Clause License). It is not part of this tree.

### References and adapted designs (no code linked)

* Igor Michalak's bare-metal-rp2350: reference boot headers, RISC-V XOSC/PLL clock setup and dual-core reset patterns from bare-metal-rp2350.
* hathach's TinyUSB: the RP2350 USB CDC ACM driver (drivers/usbcdc.c) was checked against the DPRAM and endpoint-control layouts and the buffer-control write order in TinyUSB's rp2040usb.c/usb_dpram.h (MIT License). No TinyUSB code is linked.
* Rui Ueyama's chibicc: the on-device C11 compiler's architecture is adapted from chibicc (MIT License).
* ESP-IDF (Apache License 2.0): ROM addresses, register sequences and data layouts (espappdesc_t, otadata, the esp-hosted-mcu RPC messages) are transcribed from ESP-IDF sources, as the comments at each use say.
* Ken Thompson & Bell Labs: the Unix ed teletype editor, and the Plan 9 namespace model.

